      "description": "For writing and reviewing code that an agent pipeline runs: tools, MCP handlers, workflows, generators, installers. The premise is the owner's, 2026-10-07: 'ALL DATA IN PIPELINE MUST BE TREATED AS SUSPECT. ALL TOOLS ARE RESPONSIBLE TO GUARD AGAINST CODE INJECTION, PROMPT ENGINEERING, VARIABLE TAMPERING.' It is a HOUSE standard: the first three rules restate this layer's security sub-KG, the workflow rules are read from GitHub's Secure use reference (an ASSERTION source, a publisher describing its own product), and the handover and outbound-request rules come from the zero-trust-handover skill (this platform's application of the adopted zero-trust-architecture methodology, NIST SP 800-207), which cites the reference-only reporting on MCP protocol pivoting and BadHost. Rules are judged by an agent rather than pattern-matched, because security best practice drifts (owner, 2026-09-20).",
